Hall of Great Compassion (大悲殿), Kong Meng San Phor Kark See Monastery
The Hall of Great Compassion presents a layered lateral ensemble, structured around Thousand-Armed Avalokiteśvara as the central intercessory figure, flanked immediately by protective guardians (Guan Yu and Skanda), and further enclosed by a secondary bodhisattva pair (Samantabhadra and Mañjuśrī) at the far edges of the hall. This produces a three-tier lateral grammar:
Centre: Avalokiteśvara (intercessory totality)
Inner flanks: Guan Yu + Skanda (protection)
Outer flanks: Samantabhadra + Mañjuśrī (wisdom–practice complementarity)
The result is not simple centralisation, but a concentric lateral expansion of function.

ICON descriptions
ICON₁ — Thousand-Armed Avalokiteśvara (千手千眼觀世音菩薩)
POSITION : central, axial, elevated, recessed
 SCALE : dominant
 ORIENTATION : front-facing (toward main entrance)
 FUNCTION : intercessory, soteriological, devotional, cosmological
Avalokiteśvara forms the absolute centre of the hall. The thousand-armed form encodes totalised compassion, functioning as a complete intercessory system.

ICON₂ — Guan Yu Sangharama (關羽伽藍菩薩)
POSITION : peripheral (inner-left flank)
 SCALE : subordinate
 ORIENTATION : outward-facing / lateral
 FUNCTION : protective, administrative
ICON₃ — Skanda (韋馱菩薩)
POSITION : peripheral (inner-right flank)
 SCALE : subordinate
 ORIENTATION : outward-facing
 FUNCTION : protective, apotropaic
These two form the inner protective layer, directly flanking Avalokiteśvara.

ICON₄ — Samantabhadra (普賢菩薩) (far left)
POSITION : peripheral (outer-left)
 SCALE : subordinate
 ORIENTATION : inward-facing
 FUNCTION : devotional, cosmological
ICON₅ — Mañjuśrī (文殊菩薩) (far right)
POSITION : peripheral (outer-right)
 SCALE : subordinate
 ORIENTATION : inward-facing
 FUNCTION : pedagogical, cosmological
Samantabhadra and Mañjuśrī form the outermost bodhisattva pair, traditionally representing practice (行) and wisdom (智) respectively.

