This confirms the Three Great Bodhisattvas (三大士) configuration at shrine scope and the canonical paired Buddhist guardian configuration at altar scope. Both are formally complete iconographic programmes operating simultaneously at different scopes — the previous analysis stands, with the provisional identifications now removed.

Two layered protective programmes
What the dual confirmation now reveals more sharply is that the hall encodes two simultaneous protective programmes operating at different scopes:
ALTAR SCOPE — immediate protection
  Sangharama and Skanda flanking Avalokiteśvara directly
  on the principal altar platform.
  Function: apotropaic boundary at the altar itself.

SHRINE SCOPE — soteriological structure
  Mañjuśrī and Samantabhadra in lateral altars constituting
  the bodhisattva path with Avalokiteśvara at its centre.
  Function: doctrinal articulation of the path she embodies.
These are not redundant. The guardian pair encodes the defensive enclosure of the central deity — the immediate apotropaic boundary that protects the principal sacred presence at her own altar. The bodhisattva pair encodes the structural integration of the central deity within the bodhisattva path — the doctrinal context that gives her compassion its soteriological meaning. The first protects; the second contextualizes. Both are necessary, both operate concurrently, and both subordinate to Avalokiteśvara without competing with each other because they perform their functions at different scopes of the hall's iconographic logic.

