What the dual configuration tells us
Avalokiteśvara at this hall stands at the convergence of three simultaneously operative theological structures:
1. APOTROPAIC PROTECTION
   immediate boundary maintenance at altar scope
   [Sangharama + Skanda]

2. DOCTRINAL ARTICULATION
   the bodhisattva path as karuṇā-prajñā-caryā
   [Mañjuśrī + Samantabhadra]

3. SACRED GEOGRAPHIC COMPRESSION
   three of the four great pilgrimage mountains
   [Putuo + Wutai + Emei]
She is simultaneously protected at her altar, contextualized within her doctrinal structure, and the geographic anchor of a compressed pilgrimage landscape. None of these three structures is reducible to the others, and all three are concurrently true. RFIG's interpretation rule R4 — that multiple relations within an ensemble are non-exclusive — finds one of its clearest illustrations here. The grammar correctly records all three as distinct analytical claims operating at different scopes (altar, shrine, complex), and the theological richness of the configuration emerges from simultaneity rather than from any single dimension.
The Hall of Great Compassion at Kong Meng San is, in this reading, one of the most theologically dense single-hall configurations the corpus has documented — denser, in some respects, than even the Mahavira Hall at Lian Shan Shuang Lin, because its principal deity is simultaneously the centre of an apotropaic, a doctrinal, and a geographic programme operating in concurrent layers around her.
